rgic reaction\n\nDelayed skin rashes\n\nOther\n\nNon Allergic reaction\n\nNeurological (Numbness, Tingling)\n\nDelayed Flu-like Symptoms\n\nMyucarditis\n\nOther\n\nQuestions Regarding Potential Vaccine Exemption\n\nPediatric Age-Related Concerns\n\nGeneral Anxiety about COVID-19 Vaccination\n\nMixing of COVID-19 Vaccines\n\nAdministration-Related Concers\n\nOther\n\n62\n\n（124）\n\n47 （9.4）\n\n14 （7.8）\n\n14(28)\n\n14 （2.8）\n\n1 (023\n\n901.8\n\n5（1.0）\n\n€ (0.00\n\n1008）\n\n35（7.0）\n\n74\n\n（14.8）\n\n:9(1.8\n\n901.8\n\n3（0.6）\n\n2(04)\n\n1(02)\n\n17 （3.4）\n\n60.2\n\n0 40.00\n\n27 （5.4）\n\n180\n\n（36.1）\n\n34（6.円）\n\n54\n\n（10.8）\n\n10（2.0）\n\n77（5.4）\n\n11 (22)\n\n3006）\n\n38 （7.6）\n\n1513.0）\n\n1(0 2)\n\n741.4\n\n0 40.00\n\n$（18）\n\n8（16）\n\nIgE- Immunoglobulin F, COVID-19-coronavirus disease 2019 cause by the novel coronavirus SARS-CoV-2, GI =gastrointestinal\n\nTable 3 eConsult outcome as determined by specialist\n\nglycol (PEG) (Table 2). Other common queries included vaccination safety/efficacy in the setting of various comorbidities, including autoimmune dis-\n\nease, history of venous or arterial thrombosis, and immunodeficiency.\n\nAn important category of eConsult involved possible reaction to a first dose of the COVID-19 vaccine. Commonly reported concerns included possible IgE-mediated response, delayed skin rashes, and neurologic symptoms such as paresthesia post immunization. This comprised a significant number of Consults, but was likely underrepresented as a whole given rising numbers of first doses rates administered in the latter months of our study [20].\n\nThe eConsult process did effectively alter the planned clinical path for a significant number of patients studied. 72.1% of Consults reviewed (360/499) were answered by specialist electronically without requirement for external resources or referral to other specialty (lable 3). After completion of consultation, only a small percentage of eConsult responses recommended in-person allergy assessment or another specialist referral. From a PCP perspective (Table 4), although in-person referral was initially considered in 276/499 cases, 220 of the 276 eConsults (79.7%) were ultimately managed without an in-person referral. These rates are higher than those previously documented with other specialties; 38% in a similar study of rheumatology Consults and 66% in a study of hematology eConsults [12-14]. Interestingly, PCPs used the Consult service for 181 patients when they hadn't intended to refer a patient in the first place, and 9 of those Consults lead to formal in-person consultation.\n\nDiscussion\n\nSummary & explanation\n\nOur study is the first of its kind to describe an eConsult platform as it pertains to clinical questions surrounding", "width": 1654, "height": 2205, "blocks": [{"kind": "table", "text": "199 Total 19 （3.83 Recommended referral to other specialty 30 （6.0） Recommended referral to allergy 70.4 Review of NACI guidance 8（16） Contacting col eague 10（2.0） Review of CL guidance 1302.6） Review of specialty society guidance 12 (3.9 Review of vaccine ingredients 2815.0 Review of external resources (unspecified)  Able to answer after... 7（14） Unable to answer/outside scope of practice 360（72.1） Able to answer without external resources Totals (%) Intervention", "bbox": {"x": 165.40000246465206, "y": 1525.1249912381172, "width": 634.033307865262, "height": 395.06251752376556}}, {"kind": "heading", "text": "Walter et al. Allergy, Asthma & Clinical Immunology", "bbox": {"x": 1024.561110883641, "y": 2085.5624583697977, "width": 473.22777777777776, "height": 22.968833237886283}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "(2023) 19:38", "bbox": {"x": 845.3777775664332, "y": 2085.562489001709, "width": 124.04999999999993, "height": 22.968771904707037}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Page 5 of 9", "bbox": {"x": 151.58695477191625, "y": 2085.4130244186335, "width": 114.92053442001344, "height": 23.26770105957995}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Table 2 Consults coded by question po